## Core Concepts

*   **Authentication**: The process of identifying who a user is (e.g., logging in).
*   **Authorization**: The process of determining what an authenticated user is allowed to do.

## Implementing Authorization with the `[Authorize]` Attribute

The `[Authorize]` attribute is the primary mechanism for restricting access in ASP.NET Core MVC. It can be applied at different levels.

### 1. Action-Level Authorization

Apply the `[Authorize]` attribute directly to controller action methods you want to protect. This provides granular control.

```csharp
public class HomeController : Controller
{
    // Anonymous users can access this
    public ViewResult Index() { ... }

    // Only authenticated users can access this
    [Authorize]
    public ViewResult Edit(int id) { ... }
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> When an unauthenticated user tries to access a protected resource, they are redirected to the login page with a `ReturnUrl` query parameter. This parameter holds the original URL they were trying to visit, allowing the application to redirect them back after a successful login.

### 2. Controller-Level Authorization

Apply `[Authorize]` to the controller class to protect all actions within it by default. Use the `[AllowAnonymous]` attribute on specific actions to create exceptions and allow public access.

```csharp
[Authorize] // All actions in this controller require authentication
public class HomeController : Controller
{
    // This action is an exception and is open to everyone
    [AllowAnonymous]
    public ViewResult Index() { ... }

    // This action is protected by the controller-level attribute
    public ViewResult Edit(int id) { ... }
}
```

### 3. Global Authorization

You can enforce authorization across the entire application by configuring a global filter in `Startup.cs`.

This is done in the `ConfigureServices` method by creating an `AuthorizationPolicy` that requires an authenticated user and adding it as a global `AuthorizeFilter`.

```csharp
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    services.AddMvc(options =>
    {
        var policy = new AuthorizationPolicyBuilder()
                         .RequireAuthenticatedUser()
                         .Build();
        options.Filters.Add(new AuthorizeFilter(policy));
    });
}
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **The Infinite Redirect Loop**: When using global authorization, the login and registration pages also become protected. This creates an infinite loop where the app tries to redirect the user to a login page that they aren't authorized to see. To fix this, you must apply the `[AllowAnonymous]` attribute to your `Login` and `Register` actions in the `AccountController`.

## Advanced Authorization

ASP.NET Core supports more advanced authorization strategies beyond simple authentication checks:

*   **Role-based Authorization**: Restricting access based on user roles (e.g., "Admin", "User").
*   **Claims-based Authorization**: Restricting access based on claims a user possesses.
*   **Policy-based Authorization**: Creating reusable authorization policies that decouple authorization logic from controllers.
